<%= render layout: "guides/frame", locals: { guide: @guide } do %>
  <p>
    Specialty pharmacies handle high-value, often refrigerated product, and that changes where DSCSA risk sits.
    The obligations are the same as any dispenser&rsquo;s; the stakes of getting them wrong are higher.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Check the headcount first</h2>
    <%= render "guides/qualify" %>
    <p>
      Specialty pharmacies owned by larger companies are often over 25. If yours is, the enhanced, electronic,
      package-level requirements already apply; the <%= DscsaDeadline.label %> date is not yours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where specialty pharmacies get caught</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Suspect product.</strong> High-value product is where diversion and counterfeiting show up.
        Quarantine anything suspect and investigate it with your trading partners promptly. See
        <%= link_to "handling suspect and illegitimate product", guide_path("dscsa-suspect-illegitimate-product") %>.</li>
      <li><strong>Shortage buying.</strong> A new source during a shortage must still be an authorized trading
        partner. Check the license before you buy, and keep a dated record of the check.</li>
      <li><strong>Records you can produce.</strong> In a recall or investigation you have two business days to
        produce the records you received for a specific product.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What applies today</h2>
    <%= render "guides/applies_today" %>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Cold chain and delivery records</h2>
    <p>
      DSCSA doesn&rsquo;t regulate temperature or delivery to the patient, but your manufacturer and
      limited-distribution agreements, payers and state board may. Keeping a temperature and custody record for
      each delivery is how you answer them. See <%= link_to "DSCSA and pharmacy delivery", guide_path("dscsa-pharmacy-delivery") %>.
    </p>
  </section>
<% end %>
